Now I see the issue clearly. The `haApi` function at line 309-313 doesn't include any Authorization header:

```javascript
async function haApi(path, method = 'GET', body = null) {
    const opts = { method, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } };
    if (body) opts.body = JSON.stringify(body);
    return await fetch(INGRESS_BASE + 'api/ha/' + path.replace(/^\//, ''), opts);
}
```

No `Authorization` header is set. The nginx proxy at `/api/ha/` injects `Authorization: Bearer ${HA_SUPERVISOR_TOKEN}`, so the frontend doesn't need to send it. Wait, that means the proxy IS injecting it...


But the nginx logs show 401, so the token injection isn't working. I need to check whether `HA_SUPERVISOR_TOKEN` is actually being set in the environment when nginx starts.

Looking at the startup logs, I see the message about the supervisor token being loaded, but I should trace through the actual token assignment logic in run.sh to see if it's being properly captured from the Supervisor's environment variable. I need to trace through the run.sh file to understand the complete token handling flow—specifically whether the environment variable is being set correctly and if it's available to nginx when the config gets written.

